# Apache Spark Data Processing Best Practices

## Spark Configuration and Performance
- MUST configure Spark session with appropriate memory settings for data volume
- MUST use appropriate partitioning strategies for large datasets
- MUST implement broadcast joins for small lookup tables
- MUST avoid shuffles when possible through careful data organization
- MUST set appropriate parallelism levels based on cluster resources

## DataFrame Operations
- MUST use DataFrame API over RDDs for better optimization
- MUST cache DataFrames only when reused multiple times
- MUST use appropriate data types to minimize memory usage
- MUST implement predicate pushdown for efficient filtering
- MUST use column pruning to read only required columns

## Data Quality and Validation
- MUST implement schema validation before processing
- MUST handle null values appropriately with coalesce or null-safe operations
- MUST implement data quality checks with descriptive error messages
- MUST use assert statements for critical data assumptions
- MUST log data quality metrics for monitoring

## Error Handling and Resilience
- MUST implement retry logic for transient failures
- MUST use try-catch blocks for critical operations
- MUST implement graceful degradation for non-critical failures
- MUST log detailed error information for debugging
- MUST implement circuit breakers for external dependencies

## Resource Management
- MUST monitor memory usage and implement garbage collection strategies
- MUST use appropriate file formats (Parquet preferred for columnar storage)
- MUST implement proper cleanup of temporary files and resources
- MUST use checkpointing for long-running operations
- MUST optimize file sizes for HDFS/S3 storage (128MB+ recommended)

## Partitioning and Performance
- MUST partition data by high-cardinality columns for optimal performance
- MUST avoid data skew through salting or bucketing strategies
- MUST use appropriate partition sizes (100MB-1GB recommended)
- MUST implement dynamic partition pruning for query optimization
- MUST use bucketing for frequently joined large tables

## Code Organization
- MUST separate Spark configuration from business logic
- MUST use functions for reusable transformations
- MUST implement proper logging with structured format
- MUST use configuration files for environment-specific settings
- MUST implement unit tests for transformation logic